1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,554
The brush uncovers a tablet with several lines on it, and each line begins with a name.

2
00:00:05,554 --> 00:00:11,672
They are the names of donors — women who gave money to have something built, and had their names cut into it.

3
00:00:16,233 --> 00:00:20,359
Most of the writing that survives from ancient India was produced by men.

4
00:00:20,359 --> 00:00:26,846
Inscriptions like this one are among the places where women appear in their own right, named, doing something.

5
00:00:28,633 --> 00:00:33,348
Texts, which fall into two very different groups: literature and law books.

6
00:00:33,348 --> 00:00:35,829
Inscriptions, especially donor records.

7
00:00:35,829 --> 00:00:36,822
And travellers.

8
00:00:36,822 --> 00:00:44,194
The single most important thing in this episode is keeping those groups apart, because they say different things.

9
00:00:45,133 --> 00:00:46,420
Start with the named.

10
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:51,798
The Rigveda preserves hymns attributed to women composers, Lopamudra and Ghosha among them.

11
00:00:51,798 --> 00:00:59,114
The Upanishads record Gargi questioning a philosopher in public debate and Maitreyi choosing instruction over property.

12
00:01:03,167 --> 00:01:10,307
The Therigatha is a collection of verses by Buddhist nuns — one of the earliest bodies of writing anywhere composed by women.

13
00:01:10,307 --> 00:01:16,841
And donor inscriptions at Sanchi, Bharhut, Mathura and across the south name women giving in their own names.

14
00:01:19,367 --> 00:01:22,335
Now the distinction that organises the whole topic.

15
00:01:22,335 --> 00:01:23,762
The law books prescribe.

16
00:01:23,762 --> 00:01:30,498
They tell you what their authors thought should happen — restrictions on inheritance, on remarriage, on independence,

17
00:01:30,498 --> 00:01:38,334
lowering the age of marriage, and in one much-quoted passage the claim that a woman should not be independent at any stage of life.

18
00:01:42,467 --> 00:01:43,738
Inscriptions record.

19
00:01:43,738 --> 00:01:49,305
They show women owning property, making donations, endowing temples, and acting as patrons.

20
00:01:49,305 --> 00:01:53,116
Both are evidence; they are evidence of different things.

21
00:01:57,300 --> 00:01:58,706
What changes over time?

22
00:01:58,706 --> 00:02:07,141
The broad picture most textbooks give is of narrowing: women's participation in ritual and learning more visible in the earlier Vedic material,

23
00:02:07,141 --> 00:02:11,359
more restricted in the later; property rights limited in the law books,

24
00:02:11,359 --> 00:02:17,224
with stridhana — a woman's own property — recognised but bounded; the age of marriage falling.

25
00:02:20,633 --> 00:02:22,611
But it is not one smooth line.

26
00:02:22,611 --> 00:02:26,948
The Buddhist and Jain orders admitted women, with additional rules.

27
00:02:26,948 --> 00:02:29,500
Donor inscriptions continue throughout.

28
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:33,882
And the evidence differs by region, period and social position.

29
00:02:35,833 --> 00:02:37,553
And remember who is invisible.

30
00:02:37,553 --> 00:02:41,712
Nearly all our evidence concerns women of property or of religious orders,

31
00:02:41,712 --> 00:02:46,171
because those are the women who endowed things and whose names got carved.

32
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:54,164
Women who farmed, wove, carried water or worked in households appear rarely, and usually only in passing.

33
00:02:54,164 --> 00:03:01,673
A claim about "the position of women in ancient India" is really a claim about the small minority the sources let us see.

34
00:03:05,367 --> 00:03:06,589
So, a steady decline?

35
00:03:06,589 --> 00:03:09,699
The familiar account says yes, and points to the texts.

36
00:03:09,699 --> 00:03:15,865
The criticism is that the decline narrative relies heavily on law books, which prescribe rather than describe;

37
00:03:15,865 --> 00:03:19,143
that it generalises from Brahmanical sources to everybody;

38
00:03:19,143 --> 00:03:25,609
and that inscriptions show women acting with means and independence in periods the narrative calls low points.

39
00:03:32,767 --> 00:03:38,696
A good answer gives the broad trend, says which sources support it, and then says what complicates it.

40
00:03:42,867 --> 00:03:45,493
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

41
00:03:45,493 --> 00:03:47,607
Prescription is not description.

42
00:03:47,607 --> 00:03:53,481
A law book tells you what somebody wanted; an inscription tells you what somebody did.

43
00:03:57,367 --> 00:03:58,100
Dig Quiz.

44
00:03:58,100 --> 00:03:59,494
Pause and name it.

45
00:03:59,494 --> 00:04:05,437
First: the woman who questions a philosopher in public debate in the Upanishads.

46
00:04:05,437 --> 00:04:06,251
Gargi.

47
00:04:08,367 --> 00:04:12,470
Second: the collection of verses composed by Buddhist nuns.

48
00:04:12,470 --> 00:04:13,564
The Therigatha.

49
00:04:13,564 --> 00:04:18,857
Third: the term for a woman's own property, recognised in the law books.

50
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:20,531
Stridhana.

51
00:04:20,531 --> 00:04:25,286
In Mains, a question on women's status in ancient India is common.

52
00:04:32,333 --> 00:04:39,528
Give the broad trend, separate prescriptive texts from inscriptions, and say plainly whose lives the sources actually show.

53
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:44,131
A tablet of names takes its place in the gallery.

54
00:04:44,131 --> 00:04:51,640
Next gallery is the last one: we walk the whole hall, from the first stone tool to the Kailasa temple, and put the series together.

55
00:04:53,833 --> 00:04:57,536
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

